<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merovingian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Merovingian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Merovingian dynasty was the first royal house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, ruling from the fifth century until 751. Its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> united much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. In its later period real power passed to the mayors of the palace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> deposed the last Merovingian king, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty.jpg" alt="Merovingian dynasty" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rudric · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merovingian_dynasty-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Merovingians first appear as kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the Roman army of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. The dynastic name means sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovech">Merovech</a>, a Salian king who was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childeric-i">Childeric I</a>, the first well-known Merovingian king. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a>, written in the 7th century, implies that the family descended from a sea-beast like a quinotaur. Today the tale is usually seen as an attempt to explain the name Merovech, meaning sea-bull. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> royal genealogies, the Merovingians never claimed descent from a god.</p><p>Their long hair distinguished them from other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, who commonly cut theirs short. A Merovingian whose hair was cut could not rule, and a rival could be removed from the succession by being tonsured and sent to a monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 486 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a>, a Roman military leader who competed with the Merovingians for power in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, he adopted his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clotilda-slave-ship">Clotilda</a>'s Nicene Christian faith when other Germanic tribes were largely Arian. In 507 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a>. The kingdom was treated as a single yet divisible patrimony, so Clovis's four sons divided it, and it stayed divided, with brief exceptions, until 679. The main divisions were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Aquitaine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> conquered Burgundy in 534. In 613 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Clotaire II</a> reunited the entire realm.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a>, who died in 639, is commonly seen as the last powerful Merovingian king. Later kings are called rois fainéants, do-nothing kings, and real power passed to the mayors of the palace. Pepin the Middle triumphed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tertry">Battle of Tertry</a> in 687, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a> in 732. Charles's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> deposed the last king, Childeric III, in 751, with the blessing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-zachary">Pope Zachary</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>On a king's death his property was divided among his heirs as if private. Kings appointed counts to defend, administer and judge, and the counts raised armies, which acclaimed a new king by raising him on their shields. Law depended on a man's origin: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Lex Salica</a>, first tentatively codified in 511. Theudebert I was the first to issue distinctly Merovingian coinage. Kings and queens used the church to their advantage, awarding monasteries and bishoprics to supporters, and the most characteristic Merovingian literature is the lives of saints. The main sources are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> and the Liber Historiae Francorum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Merovingian dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In this way the many kingships of the confederation ended, replaced by a single <b>Merovingian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
